00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
former Georgia House Minority Leader  Stacey Abrams is considering joining the  crowded Democratic field for president  Abrams gained national attention during  her battle to win the hotly contested  Georgia governor's race last year which  she narrowly lost she earned the support  of former President Barack Obama and  Oprah Winfrey her well received state of  the union rebuttal this year further  fueled speculation of a presidential or  Senate bid in her newly reissued memoir  lead from the outside how to build your  future and make real change Abrams  reflects on her career in politics and  business she also shares advice for  those who want to follow in her  footsteps Stacey Abrams joins us first  on CBS this morning since her book was  reissued welcome thank you for them much  so you had a lunch with Joe Biden who's  thinking about running to it was  thinking about running lunch you talked  about the presidency and what it means  absolutely what is the presidency mean  right now I think the presidency is  about reasserting who we are as a nation  our capacity for cohesion and our  ability to talk about marginalized  communities and those who are outsiders  without excluding the the majority  there's this false dichotomy right now  that you can either talk about those who  are left out or you talk about those who  feel like they're being left out but you  can't talk about both and I think one of  the vibrant parts of our conversation  was that I believe you can talk about  identity and that means you can include  everyone everyone wants to be seen and  the best politicians are the ones who  can bring people together not by  pretending we all have the same issues  but by recognizing the obstacles that  some of us face and the opportunities  that all of us want is this the you're  saying that's challenge for the nation  is this the political challenge for  Democrats running for president because  speaking about identity is important in  primaries for both parties but it can  sometimes cause parties to be defined by  those an identity conversations  happening within the clubhouse and the  rest of the country looks and says oh  they're just talking to themselves I  actually push back on that because I  think we have to recognize that  identities pervade all of us we each  come to the space with our histories and  our challenges the worst political  spaces are the ones where you think  everyone has the exact same narrative  and everyone has the exact same  challenges that doesn't exist in a  household let alone in a nation when we  are best it's when we acknowledge that  everyone faces different obstacles but  we all want the same thing we want  education we want Economic Security we  want health care the issue is having  leaders of both parties who understand  that there are different obstacles and  different barriers and they have plans  for how you get there but they never  forget that we're all going in the same  direction during that lunch with the  vice-president let me ask you did the  conversation of a potential future  ticket between the two of you with Vice  President you being his vice president  on the ticket we talked about a lot of  things but that was not the core issue  okay what Joe Biden's record has come  under a lot of scrutiny lately his  position on school desegregation his  vote during the Clarence Thomas  confirmation his position on a new need  Anita Hill last night he said I wish I  could have done something to get her a  fairer hearing is that legitimate  scrutiny did you talk to him about that  does that matter in 2020 so I'm having  lunch and coffee and drinks and water  with everyone running for president  right who is willing to talk to me and  part of the ability to do that is that  I'm not going to talk about what we  talked about I will give broad  brushstrokes but here's an as a  politician but here's what I would say  in my book wheat from the outside I talk  about the fact that we make mistakes  based on the information we have at the  time and one of our responsibilities is  to create space for people to understand  that mistakes happen and that you can  grow and what I hope anyone running for  president will do is acknowledge  mistakes made talk about what you've  learned and then apply those new lessons  to how you intend to lead let me ask you  about the bar report because you gave an  interview where you commented on it bars  interpretation of the Moller report  right and you said that I tend to think  it's like having your brother summarize  your report card to your parents we  should be deeply suspicious especially  since he had 12 tardies in at least  three times ditching class what are you  trying to say with that I'm saying that  it is inappropriate for us to assume we  know what the mole report says until we  can read them all the report having  someone whose job it is  not simply to he's the Attorney General  but he became Attorney General in part  because he chastised Muller during the  earlier process I do not think we can  assume that what he is reported in his  summary is an accurate summary of the  report but the best way to know what was  said in the report is to read the report  and my belief is until we've seen the  report we don't know what it says  one quick mother piece of political  business you said you've been meeting  with and having drinks with everybody  who's thinking about running for  president when you drink alone  are you having somebody running for  president is a good question yes we have  Singlish breakfast tea together in the  morning i am deeply appreciative of  those who have encouraged me to run for  a variety of offices I will say the  presidency wasn't top of mind to begin  with but I think that the success I had  in our election transforming the  electorate the work I've done as a  business leader as a civic leader as a  political leader positions me to be just  as capable of becoming the President of  the United States as anyone running my  responsibilities though is to make sure  I'm running for the right reasons and at  the right time and this is not the  conversation I was having with myself  last year what was the most important  takeaway for you having lost that that  the governor's race what was the most  important I would like to say I did not  win the governor's race  and here's why I see a distinction part  of how leadership evolves especially for  those of us who are from outside the the  normative space is that we have to  characterize what happened I was not  successful in part because I think there  was mismanagement and malfeasance in the  election process but I also have an  obligation to think about what I can do  better next time and in my mind the  first responsibility was to not concede  because that validated a system that I  do not believe was proper but I also  have the responsibility to take action  because it's insufficient to simply  complain about what you don't like if  you're not doing something to fix it and  that's why I launched fair fight action  it's why we've just launched fair count  which is looking at the census process  my job and my takeaway is that I have  more work to do and my next  responsibility is to figure out how that  plays out in the electorate there are a  number  women there are a number of minority  candidates running for the Democratic  nomination is this the year one of them  ends up being the nominee I believe so  and for me that the the conversation  that led to me writing lead from the  outside is that we have to start  evolving what the face of leadership  looks like but that means we have to  have practical tips it's not enough to  dream that you want to be things you  have to have a pathway there and what  I've tried to do in this book is not  just talk about my life because I'm  vaguely interesting but more than  vaguely interesting but really I want to  talk about how I made mistakes how I  figured out solutions how I hacked  opportunity because the way we get to a  changing and more vibrant representation  of who we are is that people who don't  see themselves as part of the  conversation find that they can be part  of the conversation and did we mention  you're also a romance novelist there's  so much to talk about Daisy Abrams thank  you for your time so my lead from the  outside is on sale right now

